      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Stop-motion animation reached new heights in the late 2000s, with Coraline marking a watershed moment for mature animated storytelling. The film's handcrafted approach, using 3D-printed facial components for unprecedented expression range, influenced later works like <a href=\"/movie/3933\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"3933\">Corpse Bride</a> (2005) and <a href=\"/movie/458253\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"458253\">Missing Link</a> (2019). The psychological depth of Coraline's story, adapted from Neil Gaiman's novella, proved animation could tackle complex themes of parental relationships and identity while maintaining technical excellence."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's visual style draws from German Expressionism and Victorian gothic, creating a unique aesthetic that influenced later dark fantasy animations like <a href=\"/movie/228326\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"228326\">The Book of Life</a> (2014) and Over the Garden Wall (2014). Its use of 3D technology in stop-motion photography created unprecedented depth, while maintaining the handcrafted charm that makes the medium so distinctive. SUBHEAD: The Evolution of Dark Children's Animation"
      },
      {
        "type": "movies",
        "movies": [
